-names></name><name><surname>Baker</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Beatty</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Woychick</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>1991</year><article-title>The variability in activity of the universally expressed human cytomegalovirus immediate early gene 1 enhancer/promoter in transgenic mice.</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Res</source><volume>19</volume><fpage>6205</fpage><lpage>6208</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1956779</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005308-Boshart1"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Boshart</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Weber</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Jahn</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Dorsch-Hasler</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Fleckenstein</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1985</year><article-title>A very strong enhancer is located upstream of an immediate early gene of human cytomegalovirus.</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>41</volume><fpage>521</fpage><lpage>530</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2985280</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005308-DaddarioDiCaprio1"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Daddario-DiCaprio</surname><given-names>KM</given-names></name><name><surname>Geisbert</surname><given-names>TW</given-names></name><name><surname>Stroher</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name><name><surname>Geisbert</surname><given-names>JB</given-names></name><name><surname>Grolla</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Post-exposure protection against Marburg haemorrhagic fever with recombinant vesicular stomatitis virus vectors in non-human primates: an efficacy assessment.</article-title><source>Lancet</source><volume>367</volume><fpage>1399</fpage><lpage>1404</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16650649</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005308-Volchkov1"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Volchkov</surname><given-names>VE</given-names></name><name><surname>Becker</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Volchkova</surname><given-names>VA</given-names></name><name><surname>Ternovoj</surname><given-names>VA</given-names></name><name><surname>Kotov</surname><given-names>AN</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>GP mRNA of Ebola virus is edited by the Ebola virus polymerase and by T7 and vaccinia virus polymerases.</article-title><source>Virology</source><volume>214</volume><fpage>421</fpage><lpage>430</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8553543</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005308-Stemmer1"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stemmer</surname><given-names>WPCCA</given-names></name><name><surname>Ha</surname><given-names>KD</given-names></name><name><surname>Brennan</surname><given-names>TM</given-names></name><name><surname>Heyneker</surname><given-names>HL</given-names></name></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>Single-step assembly of a gene and entire plasmid from large numbers of oligodeoxyribonucleotides.</article-title><source>Gene</source><volume>164</volume><fpage>49</fpage><lpage>53</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7590320</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005308-Kobinger2"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kobinger</surname><given-names>GP</given-names></name><name><surname>Feldmann</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhi</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Schumer</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Gao</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Chimpanzee adenovirus vaccine protects against Zaire Ebola virus.</article-title><source>Virology</source><volume>346</volume><fpage>394</fpage><lpage>401</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16356525</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005308-Rao1"><label>28</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rao</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Matyas</surname><given-names>GR</given-names></name><name><surname>Grieder</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Jahrling</surname><given-names>PB</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Cytotoxic T lymphocytes to Ebola Zaire virus are induced in mice by immunization with liposomes containing lipid A.</article-title><source>Vaccine</source><volume>17</volume><fpage>2991</fpage><lpage>2998</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10462234</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests: </bold>The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.</p></fn><fn fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding: </bold>Financial support was received from the following sources: The Public Health Agency of Canada and the Chemical, Biological, Radiological or Nuclear Research and Technology Initiative (grant #CRTI-06-0218RD awarded to GPK). The funders had no role in study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, or preparation of the manuscript.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>